<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Seljuk Emirate of Damascus</h1><p class="st-meta">1095 CE – 1104 CE · capital Damascus</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Damascus share of Tutush's divided realm, ruled by his son Duqaq while the First Crusade marched past.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutush-i">Tutush I</a> was killed in 1095, his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duqaq">Duqaq</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> with the support of the city's garrison and the commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughtekin">Tughtekin</a>, who became his atabeg, or guardian. His brother Ridwan held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, and the two became bitter rivals.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duqaq">Duqaq's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> covered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hauran">Hawran</a> and parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beqaa-valley">Beqaa</a>. Its policy was dominated by the feud with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, so neither brother offered serious resistance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a>. Duqaq joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerbogha">Kerbogha's</a> relief army at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-antioch">Antioch</a> in 1098, which was defeated, and fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-i-of-jerusalem">Baldwin of Boulogne</a> on the coast in 1100. Administration was in the hands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughtekin">Tughtekin</a> and the city's notables.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duqaq">Duqaq</a> died in 1104. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughtekin">Tughtekin</a> briefly ruled in the name of his infant son and then on his own account, founding the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/burid-emirate-of-damascus">Burid Emirate of Damascus</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1095 CE – 1104 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:81.49%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1095 CE – 1104 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Damascus</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Duqaq, Tughtekin</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Turkic, Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Duqaq's Damascus</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1100&amp;focus=seljuk-emirate-of-damascus" aria-label="Seljuk Emirate of Damascus on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5607 962 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Seljuk Emirate of Damascus lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5927 1218L5927 1218M5927 1218L5927 1218"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5927 1218h0M5927 1218h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5927 1218h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5927 1218h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Damascus. <a href="/atlas?year=1100&amp;focus=seljuk-emirate-of-damascus">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
